  "response": "# Contract split-brain: `User` and `Invoice` are each declared twice and the copies disagree\n\nI didn't run `crimes scan` myself or open the source files. Everything below comes from the scan JSON you provided.\n\n**crime_00026 (`Invoice`) and crime_00010 (`User`).** Each record has one declaration in `src/api/state.ts` and another in `src/billing.ts`. The `api` copy is a stripped-down version of the `billing` copy. Every disagreement is a field that `billing.ts` has and `api/state.ts` lacks:\n\n| Record | `src/api/state.ts` | `src/billing.ts` | Missing from the `api` side |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Invoice` | L9, 2 fields | L19, 11 fields | `createdAt`, `currency`, `discountCents`, `subtotalCents`, `taxCents`, `userId` |\n| `User` | L4, 2 fields | L4, 5 fields | `createdAt` |\n\nBoth pairs were matched with confidence 0.82 because the 2 shared fields make up 100% of the smaller declaration. As the scan puts it: \"Nothing in the build checks the two declarations against each other.\"\n\n## Which file an agent adding a field would edit\n\n**Most likely `src/billing.ts`.** It holds the full declarations: 11 fields against 2 for `Invoice`, and 5 against 2 for `User`. It's also where the invoice logic lives (`generateInvoice`, L50–253). It has test coverage too (`test_gap` 0 there, 0.5 for `api/state.ts`), so an agent's checks will pass after editing it. An agent searching for `interface Invoice` will treat the 11-field version as the real one.\n\n## What silently breaks if it edits only that file\n\n- **The `api/state.ts` copy falls further behind with no error.** TypeScript compares types by structure, so a `billing` `Invoice` with the new field still fits the 2-field `api` `Invoice`. It compiles, and the extra field is ignored. Any `api/state.ts` code typed against its own `Invoice` or `User` never sees the field. That includes the module-level `users: User` (L24) and `invoice: Invoice[]` (L27). The agent gets no prompt to update it.\n- **This has already happened at least six times.** The `api` `Invoice` has no `userId` (identifier), no money fields at all (`currency` and the `*Cents` fields), and no `createdAt`. The next added field becomes number seven.\n- **A limit on this:** `blast_radius` is 0 for both findings. The scanner found nothing importing `api/state.ts`, so I can't name a consumer that breaks today. The danger is the stale contract itself: the next consumer that imports from `api/state.ts` gets a type missing the money and identity fields.\n\n## Where this sits relative to the structural findings\n\n**Above them, based on `scores.agent_risk`:**\n\n| Finding | `agent_risk` |\n|---|---|\n| crime_00010 `User` contract drift | **0.37** |\n| crime_00026 `Invoice` contract drift | **0.37** |\n| crime_00033 `generateInvoice` large function | 0.13 |\n| crime_00036 `billing.ts` large file | 0.07 |\n\nThe drift findings score about 2.8× the large function and about 5× the large file. That fits the hazard. A 204-line function or a 310-line file is hard for an agent to read, but it's visible and the agent's own edit stays local. Contract drift makes a correct-looking edit wrong somewhere the agent never opened.\n\nTwo caveats:\n- **Severity points the wrong way here.** `generateInvoice` has severity 0.9, the same as the `User` drift, and the file is the largest in the repo. Ranking by severity or line count would put the structural findings level with or above the drift.\n- **The drift isn't the top finding overall.** By `agent_risk`, these two are tied for 10th. Nine findings score higher, from `direct_date` in `src/date.ts` (0.45) down to the 0.38 time-zone parse. So it comes before the structural work, not before everything.\n\n## Two quirks in the scan output\n\n- crime_00010's rationale says \"the disagreement is about the field's type, not just its optionality.\" The evidence shows a field present on one side only, which isn't a type disagreement. The severity boost for that reason looks unearned.\n- The suggested fix `type Invoice = Invoice` can't be written as-is because the names are the same. You'd need `import type { Invoice } from \"../billing\"` in `api/state.ts`, or `Pick<Invoice, …>` for a deliberate subset. Whichever you choose, make it in one change that also settles the six missing fields.",
